Perceived Depth, Formal Complexity, and Epistemic Validity: A Measurement-Theoretic Framework and Operationalization Program for the DEPTH-X Protocol (Final Theoretical Version)

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

---

Abstract

This chapter presents the final theoretical formulation of the DEPTH-X protocol. It makes no empirical claims: no experiments, physical tests, or code training were conducted, and no data are reported. The contribution is a measurement-theoretic framework that separates three quantities frequently conflated in evaluations of large language models: the depth attributed by an evaluator, the formal complexity of a text, and the epistemic quality of its reasoning. Perceived depth is decomposed into a bias parameter and a sensitivity parameter d', following the bullshit-receptivity tradition. Formal complexity is operationalized with established dependency-based and concept-based metrics, and its relational component is defined as a pair of graph density and independently rated coherence, with a mandatory density-matched discriminant-validity contrast. Epistemic quality is split into process-indexed and outcome-anchored components, the latter requiring evidence external to the evaluating system. The central hypotheses are reformulated as a proposed 2\times2 factorial crossing of form and verified reasoning quality, with per-item logical-equivalence verification, stratified stimulus sampling, and mixed-effects estimation. The Pearl hierarchy is treated as a task-conditioned competence dimension with explicit scope conditions on the complexity result. Refutation conditions are stated. The chapter concludes that the framework is evaluable but not yet evaluated; its next step is preregistration and pilot testing, not further theoretical elaboration.

Keywords: perceived depth; construct validity; nomological network; bullshit sensitivity; formal complexity; epistemic quality; Pearl hierarchy; mixed-effects models; DEPTH-X

---

1. Introduction

The evaluation of large language models has produced three salient findings whose theoretical relationship remains unclear. Models overestimate the profundity of nonsensical statements, and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) increases this bias rather than correcting it. Models exhibit large accuracy swings under content-preserving perturbations of form, including option length and problem type. Causal reasoning benchmarks reveal differential and non-monotonic failures across the three rungs of Pearl’s hierarchy. These findings are often read as symptoms of a single underlying dependence on surface cues. That inference is not licensed by the evidence.

The DEPTH-X framework was constructed to separate the quantities involved before any synthesis is attempted. Its first formulation established a conceptual distinction between perceived depth D_p, structural complexity \mathbf{C}, and epistemic quality Q_e, and refused to scalarize them. Its second formulation imported a measurement-theoretic vocabulary, decomposed D_p into bias and sensitivity, operationalized \mathbf{C} with named metrics, split Q_e into process and outcome components, and reformulated the hypotheses as designs. The present chapter integrates the literature-supported proposals and the critical advice that followed, and states the framework in its final theoretical form.

Three commitments govern the chapter. First, no empirical work is reported. The designs described are proposed protocols, not executed studies. Second, every construct is defined by its validation regime, not by a single instrument. Third, the framework specifies what would refute it. A framework that cannot state its falsifiers is not a research program.

The chapter proceeds as follows. Section 2 states the measurement-theoretic commitments and the nomological network. Section 3 decomposes D_p. Section 4 operationalizes formal complexity. Section 5 separates process-indexed from outcome-anchored Q_e. Section 6 states the hypotheses and the proposed design. Section 7 treats the Pearl hierarchy with its scope conditions. Section 8 states refutation conditions. Section 9 summarizes the contribution and its limits.

