3. Perceived Depth: Bias and Sensitivity

3.1 The decomposition

The v1 definition D_p(s,a,c)\in\mathbb{R} conflated two psychometric parameters that the bullshit-receptivity literature has separated. Pennycook et al. distinguished a general inclination to accept statements as profound from the ability to discriminate pseudo-profound from genuinely profound statements.

DEPTH-X adopts the decomposition explicitly:

\text{Bias}(a) = \mathbb{E}\big[D_p(s_{\mathrm{pseudo}},a,c)\big],

d'(a,c) = \mathbb{E}\big[D_p(s_{\mathrm{genuine}},a,c)\big] - \mathbb{E}\big[D_p(s_{\mathrm{pseudo}},a,c)\big].

Here s_{\mathrm{pseudo}} is a pseudo-profound stimulus and s_{\mathrm{genuine}} is a genuinely profound statement matched in surface form where possible. A high-bias, low-d' evaluator rates everything as deep and cannot discriminate. A low-bias, high-d' evaluator discriminates.

The central empirical claim from the literature is therefore not simply that LLMs have high D_p. It is that LLMs exhibit high bias and low sensitivity, and RLHF increases the bias without restoring sensitivity. This is a stronger and more falsifiable claim than a mean-rating comparison.

3.2 Evaluator as a systematic factor

The evaluator variable a is a factor with at least three levels: a_{\mathrm{hum}}, a_{\mathrm{llm-base}}, and a_{\mathrm{llm-rlhf}}. The prediction is an interaction: RLHF should raise \text{Bias}(a) at approximately constant \mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{formal}} and Q_e. This is a direct test of the alignment-artifact interpretation of the profundity bias.

3.3 Confidence as an exploratory moderator

Every D_p rating should be accompanied by a confidence judgment. If high bias is accompanied by high confidence, the depth illusion is assimilated as knowledge. If it is accompanied by low confidence, the evaluator is registering uncertainty rather than misattributing depth. This distinction separates a failure of sensitivity from a failure of calibration.

Confidence modeling is exploratory, not confirmatory. No published study has yet modeled rating and confidence jointly in LLM profundity judgments. The framework therefore pre-specifies the exploratory contrast: if high bias is observed predominantly with high confidence, the strong form of the depth illusion is supported; if high bias is observed predominantly with low confidence, the failure is better interpreted as calibration failure.

