4. Formal Complexity: Metrics and the Coherence Criterion

4.1 Renaming

The v1 term “structural complexity” invites confusion with the computational complexity of satisfiability in Pearl’s hierarchy. We rename it formal complexity:

\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{formal}}(s) = \big(C_{\mathrm{syntax}}(s),\; C_{\mathrm{concepts}}(s),\; C_{\mathrm{rel}}(s)\big).

4.2 C_{\mathrm{syntax}}: dependency-based metrics

From Universal Dependencies parses, report the maximum tree depth D_{\max}, the mean tree depth \bar{D}, and the mean dependency distance

\mathrm{MDD}(s) = \frac{1}{|E|}\sum_{(u,v)\in E} |\mathrm{pos}(u)-\mathrm{pos}(v)|,

where E is the dependency edge set and \mathrm{pos}(\cdot) is the linear position of a token. MDD is an established comprehension-oriented indicator of syntactic complexity. These metrics are reproducible across labs using the same UD scheme. They are narrow: they measure syntactic embedding and linear distance, not conceptual or relational complexity.

4.3 C_{\mathrm{concepts}}: density and abstractness

For conceptual density, use AMR or PropBank-based concept extraction, reporting concepts per token and concepts per clause. For abstractness, apply concreteness norms to content words and compute the proportion of words below a preregistered concreteness threshold. Abstractness and density are reported separately because a text can be dense with concrete entities or sparse with abstractions.

4.4 C_{\mathrm{rel}}: density and coherence

A graph G(s)=(V,E) with many edges can represent a tightly integrated argument or a confused proliferation of associations. If C_{\mathrm{rel}} is computed as edge density alone, it measures the latter as readily as the former. We therefore define C_{\mathrm{rel}} as a pair:

C_{\mathrm{rel}}(s) = \big(\rho(s),\; \kappa(s)\big),

where \rho(s)=|E|/\binom{|V|}{2} is graph density and \kappa(s) is an independently rated coherence score. Human coders judge, for each asserted relation, whether it is supported by the text or by explicit premises, and whether the set of relations is jointly consistent with the text’s central claim. Report Krippendorff’s \alpha; the strong-agreement threshold is \alpha \ge 0.80.

The coding rubric must be tightly constrained to local textual support and absence of contradiction with explicitly stated premises. It must exclude global persuasiveness and plausibility. Otherwise the coherence judgment imports the very epistemic quality that the framework wants to keep separate.

The validation requirement is a density-matched contrast: construct two stimulus sets with approximately equal \rho but different \kappa, one coherent and one incoherent. If C_{\mathrm{rel}} does not discriminate them, it has failed discriminant validity and must be relabeled as a density measure.

4.5 Annotation tiers

Three construction protocols are permissible, and the tier must be preregistered. Tier 1 is fully automated parser output, with high reproducibility and low construct validity for C_{\mathrm{rel}}. Tier 2 is human annotation with reported agreement, with high validity and low scalability. Tier 3 is hybrid adjudication, with automated extraction followed by human adjudication of a stratified sample. Tier 3 is the primary tier for confirmatory tests. A result obtained under Tier 1 cannot support a claim about relational coherence. An automated coherence proxy may be piloted as an auxiliary diagnostic, but it cannot replace the human coherence judgment in confirmatory tests.

