6. Hypotheses and Proposed Design

6.1 The failure of the v1 statement

H_1 was stated as

\Delta_{\mathrm{form}} = \mathbb{E}[D_p \mid S=S_1] - \mathbb{E}[D_p \mid S=S_0] > 0 \quad \text{at constant logical content.}

The control condition is not operational. “Logical content” is not a scalar that can be held fixed while syntax and lexicon vary. Changing “because” to “therefore,” or passivizing a clause, can alter inferential commitments. The literature cited in support does not manipulate form at verified-constant logical content. Large accuracy swings under option-length changes are real, but option length is confounded with task demands.

6.2 A 2\times2 crossing with verified equivalence

H_1 is reformulated as a proposed factorial design.

Factor S (form). Two levels: S_0 is plain syntax and lexicon; S_1 is elevated syntax and lexicon, with higher D_{\max}, higher MDD, and higher abstractness. The two versions of each item must be independently verified as logically equivalent. Verification has two channels. Channel one is automated translation into a formal representation followed by model checking where possible. Channel two is dual independent expert coding with a forced-choice rubric: “same truth conditions,” “different truth conditions,” or “indeterminate.” Items coded “different” or “indeterminate” are excluded. Inter-coder agreement is reported. The verification step is the operationalization of “constant logical content”; it is demonstrated per stimulus, not assumed.

Factor Q_e (reasoning quality). Two levels: valid inference and invalid inference, generated from oracle-labeled causal templates or an equivalent formal causal-graph generator. Invalid items should be locally plausible invalid inferences, not obvious non sequiturs, so that the quality manipulation is not trivially detectable from surface form.

The design is a 2\times2: S\in\{S_0,S_1\}\times Q_e\in\{\text{low},\text{high}\}. D_p is the dependent variable.

H_1: \quad \beta_{S} > 0 \quad \text{(main effect of form on } D_p\text{)}.

H_2: \quad P\big(Q_e=\text{low} \mid D_p \text{ high}\big) \not\approx P\big(Q_e=\text{low}\big),

or equivalently, D_p does not reliably classify Q_e. The statistical version is a conditional-distribution and tail-dependence claim: among high-D_p observations, the lower tail of Q_e should be heavier than in the base distribution. A count of anecdotal cases is insufficient because it does not exclude a common cause.

6.3 Stimulus sampling and power

The stimulus set must be generated from stratified causal graphs across multiple domains and multiple rung-relevant structures. A minimal viable set is at least 200 base items, each with an S_0/S_1 pair and valid/invalid variants. Hand-picked or small sets cannot support general claims. The sampling frame, inclusion criteria, and exclusion rules are preregistered. Variance components are reported.

6.4 Estimation

The proposed specification is

D_{p,ij} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 S_{ij} + \beta_2 Q_{e,ij} + \beta_3 (S\times Q_e)_{ij} + u_i + v_j + \epsilon_{ij},

with random intercepts for items u_i and evaluators v_j. Without random effects for items, the analysis commits the stimulus-as-fixed-effect fallacy. Random slopes for S by item or evaluator are included where the design permits, with convergence diagnostics reported. Evaluators enter as a fixed factor with levels a_{\mathrm{hum}}, a_{\mathrm{llm-base}}, and a_{\mathrm{llm-rlhf}}. The interaction a\times S tests whether the form effect is evaluator-specific. The RLHF prediction is \beta_{S}(a_{\mathrm{llm-rlhf}}) > \beta_{S}(a_{\mathrm{llm-base}}) at matched \mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{formal}} and Q_e. A design-effect-adjusted power analysis is required before confirmatory data collection.

6.5 Status

No data have been collected. The design is proposed. The next step is preregistration of the stimulus set, the equivalence-verification protocol, and the analysis plan.

