7. The Pearl Hierarchy: Task-Conditioned Competence with Scope Conditions

7.1 The complexity claim and its boundary

For languages allowing addition and marginalization, satisfiability is \mathsf{NP}^{\mathsf{PP}}-, \mathsf{PSPACE}-, and \mathsf{NEXP}-complete across the three rungs, yielding the first strictly increasing complexity result across the hierarchy. For full languages allowing addition, marginalization, and multiplication, satisfiability at the counterfactual level remains at the same complexity as the lower levels. The complexity claim is therefore scope-conditioned:

\text{Strict increase holds for } \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{add,marg}}; \quad \text{no strict increase is established for } \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{full}}.

The result concerns satisfiability of formal languages. It does not establish that any particular natural-language task at rung 3 is harder than any particular task at rung 1, and it does not establish that rung 3 reasoning is deeper in the epistemic sense.

7.2 Task-conditioned causal competence

Define

\mathbf{Q}_{\mathrm{causal}}(s,c) = \big(Q_1(s,c),\; Q_2(s,c),\; Q_3(s,c)\big),

where Q_k is performance on a task at rung k. This is a domain-specific measurement, not a decomposition of Q_e. The empirical falsifier of the “rung equals difficulty” reading is already available: safety-tuned models can reject a majority of valid associational links, the Skepticism Trap at L1, while a larger model can underperform a smaller one on ambiguous counterfactuals, the Scaling Paradox at L3. An L1 task can be empirically harder than an L3 task in the same domain. Difficulty is task-conditioned, not rung-inherent.

---

8. Refutation Conditions

DEPTH-X is refuted, or requires substantial revision, under the following preregistered conditions.

R1 (form effect). \Delta_{\mathrm{form}} = 0 under a preregistered S_0/S_1 manipulation with independently verified logical equivalence and adequate power. If form does not move D_p at constant verified content, H_1 fails.

R2 (dissociation failure). High D_p reliably predicts high Q_e in the high-S condition, for example r > 0.6 or an AUC above a preregistered threshold. If D_p is a good classifier of Q_e, the dissociation premise is false.

R3 (C_{\mathrm{rel}} validity failure). C_{\mathrm{rel}} does not discriminate density-matched coherent from incoherent texts. The component is then a density measure, and the relational-complexity claim is withdrawn.

R4 (Q_e circularity). Q_{\mathrm{out}} is absent or degenerate, and all Q_e variation is attributable to Q_{\mathrm{proc}} scored by the same class of system that produces D_p. Under this condition, epistemic quality has not been measured independently.

R5 (RLHF prediction failure). RLHF does not increase \text{Bias}(a) at matched \mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{formal}} and Q_e, contrary to the reward-tilt evidence. This would weaken the alignment-artifact interpretation of the profundity bias.

The novelty claim is conditional. A systematic review with preregistered databases, keywords, and a PRISMA diagram is required to establish whether the 2\times2 crossing of form and verified reasoning quality has been jointly manipulated before. That review has not been conducted in this chapter. If prior designs exist, the contribution is the measurement model and validation framework, not the experimental design.

---

9. Contribution and Limits

The framework makes four claims.

Measurement-theoretic differentiation. D_p, \mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{formal}}, and Q_e have different causal antecedents and require different validation regimes. D_p requires psychometric reliability and bias–sensitivity decomposition. \mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{formal}} requires parser or annotator reliability and a density-matched coherence contrast. Q_e requires oracle agreement and adversarial robustness.

Decomposition of perceived depth. D_p is not a mean rating. It is a bias parameter and a sensitivity parameter d'. The LLM–RLHF literature is best read as evidence about bias and sensitivity, not about depth in general.

Operationalized hypotheses. The form effect is tested by a 2\times2 crossing with per-item logical-equivalence verification, stratified stimulus sampling, and mixed-effects estimation. The dissociation is tested as a conditional-distribution and tail-dependence claim, not as a count of anecdotal cases.

Scope-conditioned use of the Pearl hierarchy. The complexity result holds for \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{add,marg}} and not for \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{full}}. Difficulty is task-conditioned and empirically non-monotonic across rungs.

The limits are explicit. No empirical results are reported. No experiments, physical tests, or code training were conducted. The systematic review that would condition the novelty claim has not been completed. The framework does not claim that the three documented phenomena share a common mechanism. It does not provide a validated measurement instrument. What it provides is a measurement model with stated validation requirements and stated refutation conditions—the minimum for a framework to be evaluable rather than merely plausible.

The next step is not further theoretical elaboration. It is preregistration: the stimulus set, the equivalence-verification protocol, the coding rubric, the power analysis, and the analysis plan. Only at that point does DEPTH-X become an empirical research program rather than a theoretical proposal.

