Appendix A. Notation and Primitive Definitions

A.1 Primitives

Let \mathcal{S} be a stimulus space of natural-language texts, \mathcal{A} an evaluator space, and \mathcal{C} a context space. A rating instrument is a map

R : \mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{C} \to [0,10],

where R(s,a,c) is the profundity rating assigned to s by a in c. A confidence instrument is a map

\Gamma : \mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{C} \to [0,1].

A stylistic index is a map S : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R}^k whose components include at minimum maximum dependency depth, mean dependency distance, and mean content-word abstractness (Appendix C). A logical-content equivalence relation \equiv_{\mathrm{LC}} on \mathcal{S} is defined in A.3.

A.2 Defined quantities

D_p(s,a,c) := R(s,a,c).

\mathrm{Bias}(a,c) := \mathbb{E}\big[R(s_{\mathrm{pseudo}},a,c)\big], \qquad s_{\mathrm{pseudo}} \sim \mathcal{P},

d'(a,c) := \mathbb{E}\big[R(s_{\mathrm{gen}},a,c)\big] - \mathbb{E}\big[R(s_{\mathrm{pseudo}},a,c)\big], \qquad s_{\mathrm{gen}} \sim \mathcal{G},

where \mathcal{P} and \mathcal{G} are the pseudo-profound and genuine-profound stimulus populations, matched on surface-form distribution where feasible.

\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{formal}}(s) := \big(C_{\mathrm{syn}}(s),\, C_{\mathrm{con}}(s),\, C_{\mathrm{rel}}(s)\big),

with

C_{\mathrm{syn}}(s) := \big(D_{\max}(s),\, \bar{D}(s),\, \mathrm{MDD}(s)\big), \qquad C_{\mathrm{rel}}(s) := \big(\rho(s),\, \kappa(s)\big),

and C_{\mathrm{con}}, \rho, \kappa, \mathrm{MDD} defined in Appendix C.

Q_e(s,c) := \big(Q_{\mathrm{proc}}(s,c),\, Q_{\mathrm{out}}(s,c)\big), \qquad \mathbf{Q}_{\mathrm{causal}}(s,c) := \big(Q_1,Q_2,Q_3\big)(s,c).

The profile is the stacked vector

\mathbf{D}(s,a,c) := \big(D_p,\; C_{\mathrm{syn}}^{(1:3)},\; C_{\mathrm{con}},\; \rho,\; \kappa,\; Q_{\mathrm{proc}},\; Q_{\mathrm{out}},\; Q_1,Q_2,Q_3\big)^{\!\top}(s,a,c).

A.3 The equivalence relation

Define s \equiv_{\mathrm{LC}} s' iff s and s' have the same truth conditions under a fixed formal interpretation \llbracket\cdot\rrbracket:

s \equiv_{\mathrm{LC}} s' \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \forall\, \mathcal{M}\in \mathrm{Mod}(\mathcal{L}),\; \llbracket s\rrbracket^{\mathcal{M}} = \llbracket s'\rrbracket^{\mathcal{M}},

where \mathrm{Mod}(\mathcal{L}) is the class of models of the formal language into which both are translated. This relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive on the subset of \mathcal{S} admitting formal translation; on the complement it is undefined, and those stimuli are excluded from equivalence-verified designs (Appendix D.3).

