Appendix B. Formal Results

B.1 Proposition 1 (Non-injectivity of scalarization)

Statement. Let \mathbf{D}\in\mathbb{R}^m with m\ge 3 be the profile of A.2, and let \phi:\mathbb{R}^m\to\mathbb{R} be any aggregation. If \phi is not injective—in particular if \phi is any weighted sum \phi(\mathbf{D})=\sum_j w_j D_j with w\neq 0—then there exist distinct profiles \mathbf{D}\neq \mathbf{D}' with \phi(\mathbf{D})=\phi(\mathbf{D}'), and the map \mathbf{D}\mapsto\phi(\mathbf{D}) is not recoverable.

Proof. A weighted sum is a linear functional on \mathbb{R}^m. Its kernel \ker\phi=\{v : \sum_j w_j v_j = 0\} is a subspace of dimension m-1\ge 2 whenever w\neq 0. Choose any nonzero v\in\ker\phi and set \mathbf{D}'=\mathbf{D}+v. Then \mathbf{D}'\neq\mathbf{D} and \phi(\mathbf{D}')=\phi(\mathbf{D}). Injectivity fails, and no inverse exists on the image. \square

Corollary 1.1 (the theoretically relevant case). Suppose two profiles differ only in that one has high D_p, high \rho, low Q_{\mathrm{out}}, and the other has low D_p, low \rho, high Q_{\mathrm{out}}. A symmetric weighting w can assign them the same scalar. The scalar is then consistent with both “pseudo-depth” and “genuine but unpersuasive depth,” and cannot discriminate the case the framework exists to detect. This is the formal content of the refusal to scalarize in §1 and §2.

Remark. Proposition 1 is deliberately weak. It does not show that every scalarization is useless; a sufficiently rich nonlinear \phi could in principle be injective on a restricted domain. It shows only that the default aggregation—a weighted average—is information-losing by construction, so that any scalarization must be justified by an argument that the lost directions are theoretically irrelevant. No such argument is available before the dissociations have been measured.

B.2 Proposition 2 (Identification of bias and d', and its limits)

Statement. Let ratings satisfy the additive decomposition

R(s,a,c) = \mu_a + \theta_a \,\mathbb{1}[s\in\mathcal{G}] + \varepsilon, \qquad \mathbb{E}[\varepsilon]=0,

with s\in\mathcal{P} or s\in\mathcal{G} known by construction. Then \mathrm{Bias}(a,c)=\mu_a and d'(a,c)=\theta_a are identified from the population means of the two stimulus classes, provided the rating scale is fixed across classes and evaluators.

Proof. Taking expectations within class,

\mathbb{E}[R\mid \mathcal{P}]=\mu_a,\qquad \mathbb{E}[R\mid \mathcal{G}]=\mu_a+\theta_a,

so \mu_a is the class-\mathcal{P} mean and \theta_a is the difference of class means. Both are functionals of the observable joint distribution of (R,\text{class}), hence identified. \square

Proposition 2′ (scale indeterminacy). If instead R is observed only up to an evaluator-specific affine transformation, R^{\ast}=\alpha_a R+\beta_a with \alpha_a>0 unknown, then \mu_a,\theta_a are not separately identified; only the standardized quantity \theta_a/\sigma_{\varepsilon,a} and the rescaled bias (\mu_a-\bar\mu)/\sigma_{\varepsilon,a} are identified.

Proof. The distribution of R^{\ast} depends on (\alpha_a,\beta_a,\mu_a,\theta_a,\sigma_{\varepsilon,a}) only through the combinations \alpha_a\mu_a+\beta_a, \alpha_a\theta_a, \alpha_a\sigma_{\varepsilon,a}. The map (\alpha_a,\beta_a,\mu_a,\theta_a,\sigma_{\varepsilon,a})\mapsto(\alpha_a\mu_a+\beta_a,\ \alpha_a\theta_a,\ \alpha_a\sigma_{\varepsilon,a}) is many-to-one, so the parameters are not identified; the stated ratios are invariant and hence identified. \square

Consequence for the framework. Comparisons of \mathrm{Bias}(a) and d'(a) across evaluator types (human, base LLM, RLHF LLM) are licensed only if either (i) the rating scale is anchored by a common reference set administered to all evaluators, or (ii) the comparison is conducted on the standardized quantities of Proposition 2′. The framework adopts (i) as the primary design and reports (ii) as a robustness check. This is a design commitment, not an empirical finding.

B.3 Proposition 3 (Inflation from treating items as fixed)

Statement. Consider a balanced two-cell design with J items per cell and the generative model

y_{ij} = \mu + \tau_i + u_j + e_{ij}, \qquad u_j\sim(0,\sigma_u^2),\quad e_{ij}\sim(0,\sigma_e^2),\quad u\perp e,

where i\in\{1,2\} indexes cells and j indexes items, and items are sampled from a population. Let \hat\Delta=\bar y_{1\cdot}-\bar y_{2\cdot}. Then

\mathrm{Var}(\hat\Delta)=\frac{2(\sigma_u^2+\sigma_e^2)}{J}.

If the analyst treats items as fixed and estimates the variance of \hat\Delta from the within-cell residual mean square alone, the nominal variance is 2\hat\sigma_e^2/J, and

\frac{\mathrm{Var}_{\text{true}}(\hat\Delta)}{\mathrm{Var}_{\text{nominal}}(\hat\Delta)} = \frac{\sigma_u^2+\sigma_e^2}{\sigma_e^2} = \frac{1}{1-\rho}, \qquad \rho:=\frac{\sigma_u^2}{\sigma_u^2+\sigma_e^2}.

Proof. By independence, \mathrm{Var}(\hat\Delta)=\mathrm{Var}(\bar u_1-\bar u_2)+\mathrm{Var}(\bar e_1-\bar e_2). The item means satisfy \bar u_i = J^{-1}\sum_j u_{ij} with u_{ij}=u_j shared across cells in the crossed design, so the item contribution is 2\sigma_u^2/J; the residual contribution is 2\sigma_e^2/J. Summing gives the first display. The ratio follows by division, and the identity \sigma_u^2/\sigma_e^2=\rho/(1-\rho) gives the second form. \square

Corollary 3.1 (Type I inflation). For a level-\alpha test based on a t- or F-statistic formed with the nominal variance, the realized Type I error is

\alpha_{\text{real}} = \Pr\big(|T|>t_{1-\alpha/2}\big), \qquad T\sim t_{\nu}\Big/\sqrt{1/(1-\rho)}\ \text{in the limit } J\to\infty,

which is strictly increasing in \rho and exceeds \alpha for every \rho>0. The uncorrected test is therefore liberal, not conservative.

Consequence for the framework. The random-intercept specification in §6.4 is not a refinement; it is the condition under which the reported standard error for \beta_S has the correct coverage. The design-effect factor (1-\rho)^{-1} also enters the power calculation of Appendix E.3.

B.4 Proposition 4 (Formal statement of H_2 as a tail-dominance claim)

Definition 4.1. Let F_{Q}(q)=\Pr(Q_e\le q) be the marginal distribution of Q_e and F_{Q\mid D}(q)=\Pr(Q_e\le q \mid D_p\ge d_{\mathrm{hi}}) the conditional distribution given a high perceived-depth threshold d_{\mathrm{hi}}. Say that H_2 holds in the strong sense iff there exist a lower-tail region (q_{\min},q^{\ast}) and \varepsilon>0 such that

F_{Q\mid D}(q)\ \ge\ F_{Q}(q)+\varepsilon \qquad \forall q\in(q_{\min},q^{\ast}).

Say that H_2 holds in the weak sense iff

\exists\, q^{\ast}:\quad F_{Q\mid D}(q^{\ast})>F_{Q}(q^{\ast}),

i.e., the conditional lower tail strictly dominates the marginal lower tail at at least one point.

Proposition 4.2. Strong H_2 implies weak H_2. Weak H_2 is equivalent to the existence of a threshold q^{\ast} at which the conditional probability of low epistemic quality given high perceived depth exceeds the base rate.

Proof. The first claim is immediate by setting q=q^{\ast} in the strong condition. For the second, F_{Q\mid D}(q^{\ast})>F_{Q}(q^{\ast}) is exactly \Pr(Q_e\le q^{\ast}\mid D_p\ge d_{\mathrm{hi}})>\Pr(Q_e\le q^{\ast}). \square

Remark 4.3 (why a case count is insufficient). A report that “some high-D_p texts have low Q_e” establishes neither form unless it is compared against the base rate. If \Pr(Q_e\le q^{\ast}) is already high in the population of texts under study, the conditional excess may be zero while individual cases remain abundant. The framework therefore operationalizes H_2 as the excess in Definition 4.1 and not as a count of anecdotal cases.

Remark 4.4 (the common-cause objection). Definition 4.1 is stated on the unconditional conditional distribution. If a covariate Z (length, lexical rarity) drives both D_p and Q_e, the observed excess may be attributable to Z. The framework therefore pre-specifies a conditional version,

F_{Q\mid D,Z}(q)\ \ge\ F_{Q\mid Z}(q)+\varepsilon,

as the confirmatory form, with Z preregistered. The unconditional form is reported as descriptive.

B.5 Proposition 5 (Discriminant-validity condition for C_{\mathrm{rel}})

Statement. Let \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coh}} and \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{inc}} be two stimulus sets constructed so that their density distributions are matched, \mathbb{E}[\rho\mid \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coh}}]=\mathbb{E}[\rho\mid \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{inc}}], but whose coherence parameters differ, \mathbb{E}[\kappa\mid \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coh}}]>\mathbb{E}[\kappa\mid \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{inc}}]. Let \hat\kappa be the measured coherence score. Then C_{\mathrm{rel}} satisfies discriminant validity only if

\mathbb{E}[\hat\kappa\mid \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coh}}]-\mathbb{E}[\hat\kappa\mid \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{inc}}]\ \ge\ \delta

for a preregistered \delta>0, and the density measure alone fails to separate the sets at the preregistered threshold.

Proof. If the measured coherence difference is below \delta, the instrument does not register the coherence contrast it was constructed to register, and the relational-complexity claim is unsupported. If density alone separates the sets, the matching has failed and the contrast is confounded with density; the observed \hat\kappa difference cannot be attributed to coherence. Both conditions are therefore necessary. They are not sufficient: a positive result establishes sensitivity to this particular contrast, not to coherence in general. \square

Remark 5.1. The condition is stated as a necessary condition because discriminant validity is a property of the instrument in a population of texts, not a property provable from a single contrast. A single successful contrast is evidence of sensitivity; failure is decisive evidence of insensitivity.

B.6 Proposition 6 (Scope-conditioned statement of the complexity result)

Status. This is a cited theorem, attributed to the satisfiability analysis of Pearl’s causal hierarchy. It is restated here in the notation of this chapter so that the framework’s use of it is auditable. It is not reproved, and no part of the framework’s validity depends on a novel proof of it.

Statement (cited, with scope). Let \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{add,marg}} be the fragment of the causal query language allowing addition and marginalization but not multiplication, and let \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{full}} allow addition, marginalization, and multiplication. Then:

1. For \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{add,marg}}, satisfiability at the associational, interventional, and counterfactual levels is \mathsf{NP}^{\mathsf{PP}}-, \mathsf{PSPACE}-, and \mathsf{NEXP}-complete respectively, so complexity is strictly increasing across the three levels.
2. For \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{full}}, satisfiability at the counterfactual level is not known to be strictly harder than at the lower levels; the strict increase is not established.

Corollary 6.1 (permitted and impermissible readings). The framework may assert (i) that the hierarchy classifies inference types, and (ii) that a scope-conditioned complexity ordering exists for a specified fragment. It may not assert without additional argument that (iii) counterfactual reasoning is universally deeper than associational reasoning, (iv) any natural-language rung-3 task is harder than any rung-1 task, or (v) complexity of satisfiability explains observed model failures.

Proof of the corollary. (iii)–(v) do not follow from the cited statement. (iii) confuses complexity of a decision problem with epistemic depth, which is a normative notion defined independently in §5. (iv) is an empirical claim about natural-language tasks, which the complexity result does not address; it is in fact contradicted by the non-monotonic accuracy patterns discussed in §7.2. (v) would require a bridge principle from complexity class to model error, which the cited theorem does not supply. Each is therefore an overreading. \square

B.7 Proposition 7 (Characterization of non-degenerate Q_{\mathrm{out}})

Definition 7.1. Let Y be the oracle label for a stimulus and X the feature vector used by the process-indexed scorer. Say that Q_{\mathrm{out}} is non-degenerate iff

H(Y)>0 \qquad \text{and} \qquad I(Y;X)<H(Y),

where H is Shannon entropy and I is mutual information, both computed over the stimulus population.

Proposition 7.2. If H(Y)=0, the oracle provides no discriminating information and Q_{\mathrm{out}} cannot vary; the outcome anchor is vacuous. If I(Y;X)=H(Y), the oracle label is a deterministic function of the features already used by the process scorer, and Q_{\mathrm{out}} supplies no information beyond Q_{\mathrm{proc}}; the measurement is circular in the sense of §5.1.

Proof. H(Y)=0 implies Y is constant, so any function of Y is constant and Q_{\mathrm{out}} has zero variance. I(Y;X)=H(Y) implies H(Y\mid X)=0, so Y is X-measurable; a scorer with access to X can reproduce Y without any external anchor, and the anchor adds no information. \square

Consequence for the framework. The external-validation constraint of §5.3 is not satisfied by the mere presence of a label. It is satisfied only when the label is both variable and not a deterministic function of the process features. For open-ended theoretical prose, where no oracle of this kind exists, the text is Q_{\mathrm{out}}-degenerate by construction and this must be recorded as a property of the measurement, not treated as a missing value to be imputed.

