Appendix C. Measurement Instruments

C.1 Syntactic metrics

Let T(s)=(V_T,E_T) be the Universal Dependencies tree of s, with V_T the token set and E_T the dependency edge set, and let \mathrm{depth}(v) be the length of the path from the root to v.

D_{\max}(s)=\max_{v\in V_T}\mathrm{depth}(v), \qquad \bar D(s)=\frac{1}{|V_T|}\sum_{v\in V_T}\mathrm{depth}(v),

\mathrm{MDD}(s)=\frac{1}{|E_T|}\sum_{(u,v)\in E_T}\big|\mathrm{pos}(u)-\mathrm{pos}(v)\big|,

\mathrm{MHD}(s)=\frac{1}{|E_T|}\sum_{(u,v)\in E_T}\big|\mathrm{depth}(u)-\mathrm{depth}(v)\big|.

MDD and MHD are reported together because MDD is linear and MHD is hierarchical; they can dissociate (a long right-branching construction has high MDD and low MHD). Parser version, UD release, and tokenization scheme are recorded as part of the measurement, since all three affect the values.

C.2 Conceptual metrics

Let V_C be the set of concepts extracted by an AMR or PropBank-based extractor and V_T the token set.

\mathrm{dens}_{\mathrm{tok}}(s)=\frac{|V_C|}{|V_T|}, \qquad \mathrm{dens}_{\mathrm{cl}}(s)=\frac{|V_C|}{|V_{\mathrm{cl}}|},

where V_{\mathrm{cl}} is the set of clauses. Let \mathrm{conc}(w) be the concreteness rating of content word w and \tau_c a preregistered threshold. Then

\mathrm{abs}(s)=\frac{\big|\{w\in W_{\mathrm{content}}(s): \mathrm{conc}(w)<\tau_c\}\big|}{|W_{\mathrm{content}}(s)|}.

Density and abstractness are reported separately and not combined into a single index, because they can dissociate and because their combination would reintroduce the scalarization refused in B.1.

C.3 Relational metrics and agreement

Let G(s)=(V,E) be the concept–relation graph. Density is

\rho(s)=\frac{|E|}{\binom{|V|}{2}} \quad \text{for } |V|\ge 2,

with \rho(s):=0 for |V|<2. Coherence \kappa(s) is the mean over relations of a binary local-support judgment,

\kappa(s)=\frac{1}{|E|}\sum_{e\in E} c_e, \qquad c_e\in\{0,1\},

where c_e=1 iff coder judgment records that e is supported by the text or by an explicitly stated premise. Global consistency with the central claim is coded separately as a single item \kappa^{\ast}(s)\in\{0,1\} and is not pooled with \kappa.

Agreement is assessed with Krippendorff’s \alpha for nominal data. For N units, M coders, and coincidence matrix entries o_{ck},

\alpha = 1-\frac{D_o}{D_e}, \qquad D_o=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{c}\sum_{k} o_{ck}\,\delta^2(c,k), \qquad D_e=\frac{1}{N(N-1)}\sum_c\sum_k n_c n_k\,\delta^2(c,k),

with \delta^2(c,k)=\mathbb{1}[c\neq k] for nominal data and n_c=\sum_k o_{ck}. The preregistered threshold is \alpha\ge 0.80, corresponding to strong agreement in the standard conventions. The threshold is a reliability criterion; it does not establish validity. A rubric can be applied with high agreement while measuring persuasiveness rather than coherence, which is why B.5 imposes a separate discriminant-validity condition and why the rubric is constrained to local support and explicit-premise consistency.

C.4 Bias, sensitivity, and confidence

Bias and sensitivity are estimated as in A.2, with the reference-set anchoring of B.2. When the reference set is administered, the scale is fixed across evaluators and the unstandardized estimates are comparable; when it is not, the standardized estimates of Proposition 2′ are reported.

The confidence moderator is modeled as an additional outcome. For rating R_{ij} and confidence \Gamma_{ij} on item i by evaluator j, the exploratory specification is

\begin{pmatrix} R_{ij} \\ \Gamma_{ij} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \beta_0^{R} \\ \beta_0^{\Gamma}\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}\beta_1^{R}\\\beta_1^{\Gamma}\end{pmatrix}S_{ij} + \begin{pmatrix}\beta_2^{R}\\\beta_2^{\Gamma}\end{pmatrix}Q_{e,ij} + \begin{pmatrix}u_i^{R}\\u_i^{\Gamma}\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}v_j^{R}\\v_j^{\Gamma}\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}\epsilon_{ij}^{R}\\\epsilon_{ij}^{\Gamma}\end{pmatrix},

with the two residuals allowed to covary. The exploratory contrast is the sign of \mathrm{Cov}(\epsilon^{R},\epsilon^{\Gamma}) within the high-bias stratum: positive covariance supports the strong reading in which the depth illusion is assimilated as knowledge, and negative or zero covariance supports the calibration-failure reading. This analysis is labeled exploratory in §3.3 and in Appendix H.

C.5 Rubric constraints

The coherence rubric is constrained by three rules, each stated as a prohibition rather than a desideratum.

1. Locality. A relation is credited as supported only if support is traceable to an explicit premise or to the text’s own assertion; world knowledge is not admissible as support.
2. No plausibility credit. A relation that is true in the world but unsupported by the text is credited as unsupported. Conversely, a relation that is false in the world but explicitly supported by the text is credited as supported. The rubric measures textual support, not truth.
3. No global-persuasiveness credit. Coders do not adjust local judgments to make the text’s overall argument appear stronger or weaker. Global consistency is recorded separately, as in C.3.

These prohibitions exist because violations would import Q_e into C_{\mathrm{rel}}, collapsing the distinction the framework is built on.

---

Appendix D. Stimulus Generation and Equivalence Verification (Protocol, Unexecuted)

D.1 Base-item generation

Base items are generated from formal causal graphs. For a graph \mathcal{G} over variables V, three item families are instantiated: an associational item querying a conditional, an interventional item querying an intervention, and a counterfactual item querying a nested counterfactual. Labels are computed by the causal-inference machinery applied to \mathcal{G}, not assigned by a judge. Invalid variants are constructed by a single local mutation—reversing one edge direction, substituting a non-ancestor for an ancestor, or replacing a conditional with its converse—so that the invalid item remains locally plausible and is not detectable from surface cues alone.

The protocol does not generate items by hand-selection. Stratification is over domain and over graph family, and the sampling frame is preregistered.

D.2 S_0/S_1 transformation operators

Two transformation families are permitted.

· Lexical elevation T_{\mathrm{lex}}: substitution of content words by higher-register synonyms matched on concreteness band where possible.
· Syntactic elevation T_{\mathrm{syn}}: promotion of a coordinate clause to a subordinate clause, addition of a nominalization, or restructuring to increase D_{\max} and MDD.

Operators that alter inferential commitment are prohibited, including substitution of “because” by “therefore,” conversion of a conditional to a biconditional, and insertion of presupposition-triggering factives. The prohibition list is part of the protocol because these operators would violate the equivalence requirement by construction rather than by accident.

D.3 Equivalence verification

Each pair (s_0,s_1) must pass two independent channels.

Channel 1 (automated). Translate both members into the formal representation \mathcal{L} and check equivalence by model checking over the model class \mathrm{Mod}(\mathcal{L}) restricted to the graph family of the item. The outcome is one of \{equivalent, non-equivalent, untranslatable\}.

Channel 2 (expert). Two coders independently assign one of three labels: same truth conditions, different truth conditions, indeterminate. Agreement is reported.

Decision rule. The pair is admitted iff Channel 1 returns “equivalent” and both coders return “same truth conditions” and coder agreement on the pair is concordant. The pair is excluded iff Channel 1 returns “non-equivalent,” or either coder returns “different truth conditions.” Pairs returning “untranslatable” or “indeterminate” are excluded and the exclusion rate is reported, because a high exclusion rate indicates that the transformation family is not equivalence-preserving at an acceptable rate and the protocol must be revised.

The decision rule is deliberately conservative. The cost is a lower yield of admissible items; the benefit is that “constant logical content” is a demonstrated property of each admitted pair rather than an assumption about the transformation family.

---

Appendix E. Statistical Model, Variance Components, and Power

E.1 Full specification

For evaluator j, item i, form level f\in\{S_0,S_1\}, and quality level q\in\{\text{low},\text{high}\},

D_{p,ij} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 S_{ij} + \beta_2 Q_{e,ij} + \beta_3 (S\times Q_e)_{ij} + u_i + v_j + w_{ij} + \epsilon_{ij},

u_i\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_u^2), \quad v_j\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_v^2), \quad \epsilon_{ij}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\epsilon^2),

with u\perp v\perp\epsilon. The term w_{ij} is included when the design is not fully crossed; in a fully crossed design it is absorbed and is set to zero.

The confirmatory tests are:

H_1: \beta_1>0, \qquad H_{1,\mathrm{alignment}}: \beta_1\big|_{a=\mathrm{rlhf}}>\beta_1\big|_{a=\mathrm{base}},

H_2: \text{the tail-dominance condition of Definition 4.1, conditional form of Remark 4.4.}

The interaction \beta_3 is reported but is not the primary test; a nonzero \beta_3 would indicate that the form effect depends on reasoning quality, which is informative but does not bear directly on H_1 or H_2.

E.2 Variance components and ICC

For the item random effect, the intraclass correlation is

\rho_{\mathrm{item}}=\frac{\sigma_u^2}{\sigma_u^2+\sigma_v^2+\sigma_\epsilon^2},

and analogously for evaluators. Both are reported for every fitted model. The design effect on the effective sample size for a treatment contrast is

\mathrm{DE}=1+(m-1)\rho_{\mathrm{item}},

where m is the average number of observations per item. The effective sample size is n_{\mathrm{eff}}=n/\mathrm{DE}.

E.3 Power approximation

For a two-sided test of a standardized effect \delta=\beta_1/\sigma_{\mathrm{tot}} at level \alpha with power 1-\pi, the required effective sample size is approximated by

n_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx \frac{2\,(z_{1-\alpha/2}+z_{1-\pi})^2}{\delta^2},

and the required raw sample size is n\approx n_{\mathrm{eff}}\cdot \mathrm{DE}. Because \mathrm{DE}\ge 1, ignoring item variance inflates nominal power. The approximation is a planning device only; it does not substitute for simulation-based power under the fitted covariance structure, which is required before confirmatory data collection.

E.4 Diagnostics and model comparison

Reported for every model: convergence status and optimizer, singularity checks on the random-effects covariance, and the profile-likelihood confidence interval for \beta_1. Model comparison proceeds by likelihood-ratio test or parametric bootstrap between the full model and a reduced model without the random slopes. A singular fit is reported as a finding about the design, not silently simplified away.

