Appendix F. Refutation Conditions, Formalized

Each condition is stated as a predicate on the population quantities, so that it can be evaluated against estimates with stated uncertainty.

R1. \beta_1=0 under an admitted S_0/S_1 manipulation with equivalence verified per D.3 and power \ge 1-\pi per E.3. Operational form: the profile-likelihood interval for \beta_1 contains zero and excludes the preregistered minimum effect \delta_{\min}.

R2. \Pr(Q_e\le q^{\ast}\mid D_p\ge d_{\mathrm{hi}})-\Pr(Q_e\le q^{\ast})\le 0 for all preregistered q^{\ast}, or the classification AUC of Q_e by D_p exceeds the preregistered threshold. Under either, the dissociation premise fails.

R3. The difference in B.5 falls below \delta, or density alone separates the matched sets. Under either, the relational-complexity claim is withdrawn and C_{\mathrm{rel}} is relabeled a density measure.

R4. Q_{\mathrm{out}} fails the non-degeneracy condition of Definition 7.1 on the population of interest, and all Q_e variation is attributable to Q_{\mathrm{proc}} computed from features correlated with those used by the profundity judge. Under this, epistemic quality has not been measured independently and the external-validation constraint is unmet.

R5. \beta_1\big|_{a=\mathrm{rlhf}}\le \beta_1\big|_{a=\mathrm{base}} at matched \mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{formal}} and Q_e. Under this, the alignment-artifact reading of the profundity bias is weakened.

R6 (novelty). If a systematic review conducted per Appendix G identifies a prior design jointly manipulating form and oracle-verified reasoning quality in a crossed factorial, the novelty claim is withdrawn and the contribution is restated as the measurement model and validation regime. This condition cannot currently be evaluated, because the review has not been executed.

---

Appendix G. Systematic Review Protocol (Specified, Not Executed)

Status. This appendix specifies a protocol. It has not been executed. No screening, extraction, or synthesis has been performed, and no claim in the main text depends on a completed review. The protocol is included so that the conditional novelty claim of §8 is auditable and so that the review can be preregistered without further design work.

Databases. At minimum: a general index, a computer-science index, and a preprint server, chosen to cover ACL- and NeurIPS-family venues, cognitive-science venues, and psychometrics venues.

Search strings (four streams). (i) pseudo-profound OR bullshit receptivity OR profundity bias AND manipulation OR style OR form. (ii) sycophancy OR reward tilt OR preference optimization AND evaluation OR bias. (iii) causal reasoning benchmark OR counterfactual AND robustness OR paraphrase OR format. (iv) construct validity AND language model benchmark.

Inclusion criteria. Peer-reviewed or preprint work reporting (a) an empirical manipulation of surface form at purportedly constant content, or (b) an oracle-labeled reasoning-quality manipulation, or (c) a validation study of an LLM evaluation instrument.

Exclusion criteria. Purely opinion pieces; work with no measurement instrument; work in which form and content are acknowledged as confounded and not separated.

Screening. Title/abstract, then full text, by two independent screeners, with disagreements adjudicated by a third. Agreement reported.

Extraction fields. Design type; whether form and reasoning quality were jointly manipulated; whether equivalence of content was verified and by what procedure; evaluator types; outcome instrument; reported reliability; reported variance components.

Synthesis. A narrative synthesis organized by the four streams, with a table of any design that crosses form and verified reasoning quality. The PRISMA flow diagram is produced at execution, not now.

Decision rule for the novelty claim. The novelty claim survives iff no included design jointly manipulates form and oracle-verified reasoning quality in a crossed factorial with per-item equivalence verification.

---

Appendix H. Register of Non-Claims

The following are explicitly not claimed by this chapter, and the appendices do not supply them.

1. No empirical results. No data were collected, no experiments run, no models trained, no physical tests performed.
2. No validated instrument. The bias and sensitivity decomposition, the formal-complexity metrics, and the process/outcome split are proposed operationalizations with stated validation requirements; they are not validated instruments.
3. No mechanism claim. The framework does not assert that the profundity bias, format sensitivity, and non-monotonic causal failure share a common cause. Their co-occurrence is treated as an open empirical question.
4. No universal difficulty ordering. The Pearl hierarchy is used as a typology of inference types with a scope-conditioned complexity result (B.6); it is not used as a scale of difficulty or depth.
5. No novelty established. The 2\times2 crossing is proposed as a design. Whether it is unprecedented is undetermined pending the review of Appendix G.
6. No completed review. Appendix G is a protocol. R6 is currently unevaluable.
7. No cross-evaluator comparability without anchoring. Per B.2, unstandardized bias and sensitivity comparisons across evaluator types are licensed only under reference-set anchoring; otherwise only the standardized quantities are comparable.
8. No inference from robustness to validity. As stated in the main text, a robustness failure is a symptom motivating the hypothesis, not a measurement of Q_e.
9. No proof of the cited complexity theorem. B.6 restates a cited result with its scope conditions; it does not reprove it, and the corollary establishes only which readings are impermissible.
10. Exploratory status of two analyses. Confidence modeling (§3.3, C.4) and any automated coherence proxy (§4.5) are exploratory. They are not part of the confirmatory test set and cannot support confirmatory claims.


